{'session_id': 'prof_3_1787066178', 'agent': 'RagChat', 'response': '<tool_call>\n{"name": "finish_tool", "arguments": {"final_output": "## What is a Walker?\n\nA walker is code that moves through the graph, triggering abilities as it enters each node:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    W["Walker: ListTasks"]:::walker -.->|spawn| root((root))\n    root -->|visit| T1["Task: #quot;Buy groceries#quot;"]\n    root -->|visit| T2["Task: #quot;Team standup#quot;"]\n    root -->|visit| T3["Task: #quot;Go running#quot;"]\n    T1 -.-o A1(("ability fires")):::ability\n    T2 -.-o A2(("ability fires")):::ability\n    T3 -.-o A3(("ability fires")):::ability\n    classDef walker stroke-width:3px,stroke-dasharray:none\n    classDef ability stroke-width:2px,stroke-dasharray:3 3\n```\n\nA walker is a mobile agent that traverses the graph, executing abilities at each node it visits. Unlike functions that you call, walkers *go to* data. They maintain state throughout their journey, making them ideal for tasks like collecting information across a graph, implementing AI agents that navigate knowledge structures, or processing pipelines where context accumulates. Spawn a walker with `root spawn MyWalker()` to begin traversal.\n\n## What is a Node?\n\nA node is structurally similar to an `obj` -- it\'s declared with the `node` keyword and its fields use `has`, just like you learned in Part 1. The difference is what the runtime does with it:\n\n```jac\nnode Task {\n    has title: str,\n        done: bool = False;\n}\n```\n\nNodes hold data and can have abilities that execute when walkers visit them. Think of nodes as "smart objects" that know when they\'re being visited and can react accordingly. Unlike regular objects, nodes can be connected via edges and participate in graph traversals.\n\n## What is an Ability?\n\nAn ability is an event handler that executes when a walker enters a specific node. It can access the node\'s data and perform actions based on it. An ability can also trigger other abilities, creating a chain of execution. Abilities are declared with the `can` keyword and specify the node type and entry point:\n\n```jac\nnode Person {\n    has name: str;\n    has age: int = 0;\n\n    can greet with Visitor entry {\n        print(f"Hello from {self.name}");\n    }\n}\n``"}\n\nWhat is an Edge?\n\nAn edge defines the relationship between two nodes. It specifies how the nodes are connected and can influence the traversal of the graph. Edges can be directed or undirected, and they can carry additional metadata such as weights or labels. Edges are declared with the `edge` keyword and specify the source and target nodes, along with any additional attributes:\n\n```jac\nnode Person {\n    has name: str;\n    has age: int = 0;\n\n    edge knows :+> Person {\n        has since: int;\n    }\n}\n\nwith entry {\n    alice = Person(name="Alice", age=30);\n    bob = Person(name="Bob", age=25);\n\n    root ++> alice;\n    alice ++> bob;\n\n    # Query connected nodes\n    people = [root-->][?:Person];\n    for p in people {\n        print(p.name);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWhat is a Graph?\n\nA graph is a collection of nodes and edges that represent relationships between them. In Jac, the graph is in-memory and managed by the runtime. It allows for dynamic and flexible data modeling without the need for external databases or complex schema definitions. Graph traversal is achieved through walkers, which follow the edges and execute the associated abilities at each node.\n\nHow do Walkers Work?\n\nWalkers are code that moves through the graph, triggering abilities as they enter each node. They maintain state throughout their journey and can interact with the nodes they visit. Walkers can spawn other walkers, allowing for complex and hierarchical traversal patterns. The `visit` keyword is used to move the walker to connected nodes, and the `here` variable represents the current node the walker is visiting. Walkers can also use the `report` statement to send data back to the code that spawned them.\n\nHow do Nodes Work?\n\nNodes are the vertices of the graph and hold data. They can be connected to other nodes via edges, forming a network of interconnected entities. Nodes can have abilities that execute when walkers visit them, providing a powerful way to model complex systems and workflows.\n\nHow do Abilities Work?\n\nAbilities are event handlers that execute when a walker enters a specific node. They can access the node\'s data and perform actions based on it. Abilities can also trigger other abilities, creating a chain of execution. Abilities are declared with the `can` keyword and specify the node type and entry point.\n\nHow do Edges Work?\n\nEdges define the relationship between two nodes. They can be directed or undirected and can carry additional metadata such as weights or labels. Edges are declared with the `edge` keyword and specify the source and target nodes, along with any additional attributes.\n\nHow do Graphs Work?\n\nGraphs are collections of nodes and edges that represent relationships between them. In Jac, the graph is in-memory and managed by the runtime. It allows for dynamic and flexible data modeling without the need for external databases or complex schema definitions. Graph traversal is achieved through walkers, which follow the edges and execute the associated abilities at each node.\n\nWhat is the Role of the Root Node?\n\nThe root node is the entry point of the graph. It serves as the starting point for all graph operations and can be referenced using the `root` keyword. The root node can connect new nodes to the graph and can also spawn walkers to traverse the graph. The root node maintains a connection to the built-in `root` object, which is used for various internal purposes.\n\nWhat is the Purpose of the `root` Keyword?\n\nThe `root` keyword is used to refer to the root node of the graph. It serves as the starting point for all graph operations and can be used to connect new nodes to the graph. The `root` keyword is also used to spawn walkers that traverse the graph. By default, the `root` keyword refers to the built-in `root` object, which is used internally by the runtime to manage the graph. However, you can also declare custom root nodes using the `node` keyword and connect them to the built-in `root` object.\n\nWhat is the Difference Between a Walker and a Function?\n\nA walker is a mobile agent that traverses the graph, executing abilities at each node it visits. It maintains state throughout its journey and can interact with the nodes it visits. Functions, on the other hand, are static pieces of code that are called and executed at specific points in the program. While walkers can be thought of as functions that go to the data, they differ in that they are designed to traverse the graph and interact with the nodes dynamically. Functions are typically used for simpler tasks that don\'t require traversal or interaction with the graph.\n\nWhat is the Importance of the `here` Variable?\n\nThe `here` variable represents the current node the walker is visiting. It allows the walker to access the data of the node it is currently at and perform actions based on it. The `here` variable is useful for implementing complex workflows and enabling dynamic behavior in the graph. By using the `here` variable, walkers can access and manipulate the data of the nodes they visit, making it easier to build sophisticated applications and systems.\n\nWhat is the Purpose of the `report` Statement?\n\nThe `report` statement is used by walkers to communicate results back to the code that spawned them. It allows walkers to send data back to the parent code, enabling the parent code to make decisions based on the results of the walker\'s traversal. The `report` statement is a key mechanism for walkers to share information and coordinate with other parts of the application.\n\nWhat is the Role of the `visit` Keyword?\n\nThe `visit` keyword is used by walkers to move to connected no