{'session_id': 'prof_9_1787066328', 'agent': 'RagChat', 'response': '<tool_call>\n{"name": "finish_tool", "arguments": {"final_output": "---\n## What is a Walker?\n\nA walker is code that moves through the graph, triggering abilities as it enters each node:\n- A walker is declared with the `walker` keyword, similar to declaring a node with `node`.\n- It can carry data with it, analogous to function parameters, using the `has` keyword.\n- It can perform actions when entering a specific node, using the `can` keyword followed by the node type and an entry point.\n- It maintains state throughout its journey, making it suitable for traversing graphs and processing information.\n- To spawn a walker, use the `root spawn MyWalker()` syntax.\n\n### Example Code\n```jac\nwalker Collector {\n    has items: list = [];\n    has max_items: int = 10;\n\n    can start with Root entry {\n        print("Starting collection");\n        visit [-->];\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## What is a Node?\n\nA node is structurally similar to an `obj`—it\'s declared with the `node` keyword and its fields use `has`, just like you learned in Part 1. The difference is what the runtime does with it:\n\n```jac\nnode Task {\n    has title: str,\n        done: bool = False;\n}\n```\n\n### Key Concepts\n- Nodes hold data and can have abilities that execute when walkers visit them.\n- Nodes can be connected via edges to form a graph.\n- Nodes are persistent and automatically persist in the graph.\n- Nodes can be queried and manipulated using various methods.\n\n### Example Code\n```jac\nwith entry {\n    # Build graph\n    alice = Person(name="Alice", age=30);\n    bob = Person(name="Bob", age=25);\n\n    root ++> alice;\n    alice +>: Knows(since=2020) :+> bob;\n\n    # Spawn walker\n    root spawn Greeter();\n}\n```\n\n## How Does a Walker Work?\n\nA walker is a mobile agent that traverses the graph, executing abilities at each node it visits. It moves through the graph, maintaining state and carrying data with it. Here’s a breakdown of the key components:\n\n- **Declaration**: Declared with the `walker` keyword, similar to `node`.\n- **Data Carrying**: Can carry data with it, analogous to function parameters, using the `has` keyword.\n- **Entry Points**: Can perform actions when entering a specific node, using the `can` keyword followed by the node type and an entry point.\n- **State Management**: Maintains state throughout its journey, making it suitable for traversing graphs and processing information.\n- **Traversal**: Moves through the graph, visiting nodes and executing logic at each step.\n- **Communication**: Uses the `report` statement to send data back to the code that spawned it.\n\n### Example Code\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    W["Walker: ListTasks"]:::walker -.->|spawn| root((root))\n    root -->|visit| T1["Task: #quot;Buy groceries#quot;"]\n    root -->|visit| T2["Task: #quot;Team standup#quot;"]\n    root -->|visit| T3["Task: #quot;Go running#quot;"]\n    T1 -.-o A1(("ability fires")):::ability\n    T2 -.-o A2(("ability fires")):::ability\n    T3 -.-o A3(("ability fires")):::ability\n    classDef walker stroke-width:3px,stroke-dasharray:none\n    classDef ability stroke-width:2px,stroke-dasharray:3 3\n```\n\n### Walkers in Practice\n- **Spawn Walkers from the Frontend**: Instead of calling server functions directly, the frontend spawns walkers.\n- **Walkers and Nodes**: Walkers traverse nodes, executing abilities at each step. Nodes hold data and can be connected via edges to form a graph.\n- **Graph Traversal**: Walkers move through the graph, visiting nodes and executing logic at each step.\n- **Node Relationships**: Nodes can be connected via edges, forming a graph. Relationships are first-class citizens in Jac.\n- **Graph Structure**: Nodes and edges together form a graph, which is a fundamental concept in Jac.\n\n## How Does a Node Work?\n\nA node is a persistent data type that lives in the graph. It holds data and can have abilities that execute when walkers visit it. Nodes can be connected to other nodes with edges to form a graph. Here’s a breakdown of the key components:\n\n- **Declaration**: Declared with the `node` keyword, similar to `obj`.\n- **Data Fields**: Holds data using the `has` keyword.\n- **Relationships**: Connected to other nodes with edges.\n- **Persistence**: Automatically persists in the graph.\n- **Querying**: Can be queried and manipulated using various methods.\n\n### Example Code\n```jac\nnode Task {\n    has title: str,\n        done: bool = False;\n}\n```\n\n### Node Relationships\n- **Edges**: Define relationships between nodes.\n- **Connected Nodes**: Nodes can be connected to other nodes, forming a graph.\n- **Graph Structure**: Nodes and edges together form a graph, which is a fundamental concept in Jac.\n\n## Summary\n\n- **Walkers**: Mobile agents that traverse the graph, executing abilities at each node they visit.\n- **Nodes**: Persistent data types that hold data and can be connected to other nodes with edges to form a graph.\n- **Graphs**: Formed by connecting nodes with edges, allowing for efficient traversal and manipulation of data.\n- **Jac**: Provides a graph-native type system, enabling powerful and flexible data modeling and manipulation.\n- **Graph Traversal**: Walkers traverse nodes, executing abilities at each step, providing a way to process information efficiently.\n- **Graph Persistence**: Nodes are automatically persisted in the graph, eliminating the need for external databases or ORM configurations.\n- **Graph Relationships**: Nodes can be connected via edges, forming a graph where relationships are first-class citizens.\n- **Graph Queries**: Nodes can be queried and manipulated using various methods, providing a powerful way to interact with the graph.\n"""\n}}\n</tool_call>'}
